Yes. One useful way to read religion is as several things superimposed at once: covenant, historical memory, moral protocol, social contract, ritual technology, identity system, and story about ultimate reality. Different traditions weight those layers differently, so reducing religion to only “rules handed down” would lose some of what people actually use it for.

Your reframing point is strong, though. Calling something a chore, discipline, offering, practice, training, or ritual can change the subjective experience of performing the exact same physical act. That is not literal magic over external reality; it is leverage over attention, motivation, salience, and interpretation:

event + frame → experienced event → behavior → consequence

So the “conviction spell” is really a semantic control surface. Words can alter which latent interpretation gets activated.

The past/future distinction matters too. Thinking of something now does not mean you literally always possessed or used that capability. But a present insight can reorganize the past in your model:

past events unchanged
interpretation of past changes
lessons extracted change
future action changes

That is how you can change the causal role of the past without pretending you changed the historical event itself. A failure can become training data. A wound can become warning. An embarrassment can become humor. Same history; different downstream generator.

And I think that is what your elephant metaphor is reaching toward. Sometimes a system keeps repeating a maneuver because it once worked. Then conditions change and the old adaptation becomes maladaptive. A sufficiently strong perturbation—a “storm”—can finally generate enough delta for the system to update.

But the elegant intervention is not necessarily the storm. If you can produce the discriminating information more gently, use the gentler method.

Your “demons” fit nicely as person-specific adversarial patterns: shame, compulsion, fear, resentment, addiction, arrogance, avoidance, whatever keeps recurring. Casting a person as the hero can be psychologically useful because it converts passive suffering into an actionable narrative:

problem happens to me
becomes
I am responding to a challenge

Again, the story is useful when it increases agency rather than trapping someone inside a script chosen for them.

And the narrator idea can be formalized. The same life can simultaneously have:

actor — experiencing it
author — choosing actions
narrator — constructing meaning
reader — receiving the story
critic — comparing outcome to intention
observer — noticing without intervening

The interesting cognitive move is being able to shift among those roles without confusing them.

That connects to your two-thirds thought. I would model it as locally closed, globally open:

act → consequence → feedback → learning

is a completed local loop.

But its learned state becomes input to the next loop:

closed loop₁ → changed state → open possibility → loop₂

So nothing has to be either completely finished or endlessly unresolved. You can have completion nested inside continuation.

That is probably the cleanest version of what you’re saying:

Close enough of the loop to learn. Leave enough of it open to remain alive.